/// Chapter 1 of the guide, written from md-docs/01-getting-started.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page GettingStarted(path: "/docs/guide/getting-started", title: "Getting started", description: "Install wf, run a first project, and learn the project layout and the config file.", layout: DocsShell("Getting started", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "1" }), blurb: "Install the compiler, make a project, and see what a build writes.", sections: [Anchor(id: "install", label: "Install"), Anchor(id: "a-first-project", label: "A first project"), Anchor(id: "the-smallest-site", label: "The smallest site"), Anchor(id: "what-a-build-produces", label: "What a build produces"), Anchor(id: "project-layout", label: "Project layout"), Anchor(id: "the-config-file", label: "The config file")], nextTo: "/docs/guide/language-basics", nextLabel: "2. {t("ch.02")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("By the end of this chapter you will have installed the compiler, built a two-page site, changed it with the dev server watching, and know where every file of a project lives.")

        Heading("Install", id: "install").h2

        Markdown("WebFluent is one binary, `wf`. Run the line for your machine; each one leaves `wf` on your `PATH`.")

        Heading("macOS and Linux", id: "macos-and-linux").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash")
            ])

        Markdown("Fetches the binary for your platform from the latest release — no Rust needed. Covers Apple Silicon and Intel Macs, and x86_64 Linux.")

        Heading("Windows", id: "windows").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.ps1 | iex")
            ])

        Heading("With Rust", id: "with-rust").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo install webfluent")
            ])

        Markdown("Builds from crates.io. This is the one that works everywhere Rust does, and it is how to install on Linux arm64, which has no prebuilt binary yet.")

        Heading("Check it", id: "check-it").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf --version")
            ])

        Markdown("If the shell cannot find `wf`, open a new terminal: the installer adds its directory to your `PATH` in your shell's startup file, which the session you ran it in has already read.\n\nBoth installers take two environment variables — `WF_INSTALL_DIR` to put the binary somewhere other than `~/.webfluent/bin`, and `WF_VERSION` (`v3.2.1`) to pin a release rather than take the latest.\n\nTo build from a clone instead, for an unreleased change or to work on the compiler itself:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "git clone https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent.git"),
                Line(command: "cd WebFluent && cargo install --path .")
            ])

        Markdown("The language server for your editor is a second binary, `wf-lsp` — see [Tooling](/docs/guide/tooling#editors) for Zed and VS Code.")

        Heading("A first project", id: "a-first-project").h2

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf init hello"),
                Line(command: "cd hello"),
                Line(command: "wf serve")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf init` writes a small application (a dashboard, a task list, a settings page, a store, a theme) so you have something to read; `wf serve` builds it, opens it at `http://localhost:3000`, rebuilds when you save, reloads the page when a build lands, and — when a build fails — draws the error over the page until the next save fixes it.\n\nTemplates: `wf init name -t spa` (the default, an interactive app), `-t static` (a marketing site, pre-rendered), `-t pdf` (a document), `-t slides` (a deck).")

        Heading("The smallest site", id: "the-smallest-site").h2

        Markdown("Replace `src/` with two files.\n\n`src/App.wf` — the shell every page shares, and the router:")

        CodeBlock(#"app {
    Navbar {
        Navbar.Brand { Text("Hello").heading }
        Navbar.Links {
            Link("Home", to: "/")
            Link("About", to: "/about")
        }
    }
    Router
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`src/pages/Home.wf` — a page that owns its route:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "Hello", description: "A first WebFluent site.") {
    state count = 0
    Container {
        Heading("Hello, world").h1
        Text("You have clicked {count} times.")
        Button("Click me").primary { on click { count = count + 1 } }
    }
}

page About(path: "/about", title: "About", description: "What this site is for.") {
    Container {
        Heading("About").h1
        Paragraph("Two pages, one shell, no framework.")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Read it top to bottom:\n\n- `app \{ … \}` is the shell: what every page sits inside. `Router` is where the current page renders. There is no route table — each `page` names its own `path:`.\n- `page Home(path: \"/\", title: …, description: …)` declares a page. `title` and `description` become the `<title>`, the meta description, the Open Graph and Twitter cards and the JSON-LD; leave them out and the build tells you.\n- `state count = 0` is a signal. `Text(\"… \{count\} …\")` reads it, so the text updates when it changes.\n- `Button(\"Click me\").primary \{ on click \{ … \} \}` — the label is the one positional argument, `.primary` is a flag (a case of the button's `tone` prop), and the block holds the click handler.\n- `Container`, `Heading`, `Text`, `Paragraph`, `Navbar`, `Link` are built-ins; [chapter 20](/docs/reference) lists all of them.\n\nSave, and the dev server shows it. `wf build` writes the finished site to `build/`.")

        Heading("What a build produces", id: "what-a-build-produces").h2

        CodeBlock(#"build/
  index.html          the page, pre-rendered (with build.ssg) or the app shell
  about/index.html    one directory per route in a static build
  app.js              the runtime this program needs, stores and components
  pages/Home.js       each page in its own chunk, loaded when its route shows
  pages/Home.css      the styles only that page reaches
  styles.css          the shared sheet: your tokens, the built-ins you used, your stylesheets
  sitemap.xml, robots.txt
  *.gz                pre-compressed copies a host can serve as-is"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Nothing in `build/` is hand-editable; it is regenerated on every build.\n\n`app.js` carries only the parts of the runtime the program reaches. The runtime is a set of feature modules — `each` for a `for`, `router` for a second page, `net` for a `resource`, `icons` for an `Icon` — and the build works out which ones the code it just wrote calls into. A page of static text carries 19 kB of runtime where the whole of it is 107 kB.\n\n`wf build --stats` shows it:")

        CodeBlock(#"  Runtime: 4 of 28 modules, 24.9 kB of 107.2 kB (before minifying)
    kept     core pages store icons
    left out motion helpers format form text when match slot each show …"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("The one thing a build cannot see is a name assembled at run time — an icon whose name arrives from an API, or `WF.*` called from a hand-written `<script>`. `\"build\": \{ \"runtime\": \"full\" \}` ships every module for that.")

        Heading("Project layout", id: "project-layout").h2

        CodeBlock(#"webfluent.app.json    the config (below)
src/
  App.wf              the app shell (read first; optional)
  pages/*.wf          pages — any file name, any nesting
  components/*.wf     components
  stores/*.wf         stores
  theme.wf            a theme, if you declare one
  translations/*.json translation files, with "i18n" in the config
  *.css               stylesheets, bundled into styles.css
  *.md                Markdown pages (chapter 16)
public/               copied to build/ as-is (images, fonts, favicon)
tests/*.wf            `test "…" { }` declarations for `wf test`"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("The compiler reads every `.wf` (or `.wfx`) under `src/` and merges their declarations into one program: a page in one file can use a component from another without importing anything. Names are global to the project, so two declarations of the same name is an error.")

        Heading("The config file", id: "the-config-file").h2

        Markdown("`webfluent.app.json` — every key is optional except `name`. What is shown here is the default, so a key you never write behaves as it reads:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "name": "hello",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "",
  "build": {
    "output": "./build",
    "output_type": "spa",
    "ssg": false,
    "split": true,
    "minify": true,
    "sourcemap": false,
    "compress": true,
    "runtime": "auto",
    "csp": false,
    "base_path": "",
    "budget": {},
    "elements": [],
    "media": { "formats": ["webp"], "widths": [480, 960, 1440, 1920], "quality": 78, "pipeline": true }
  },
  "dev": { "port": 3000, "hot_reload": true },
  "motion": { "duration": null, "easing": null },
  "meta": {
    "title": "", "description": "", "lang": "en",
    "site_url": "", "site_name": "", "image": "", "favicon": "",
    "sitemap": true, "fonts": [], "stylesheets": [], "integrity": {}
  },
  "theme": { "name": null, "dark": null, "builtin": "full", "tokens": {} },
  "i18n": { "default_locale": "en", "locales": ["en"], "dir": "src/translations" },
  "env": {},
  "public_env": []
}"#, title: "json")

        Heading("The project", id: "the-project").h3

        Table(caption: "The project") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("Default").header
                    Table.Cell("Meaning").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name`") }
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell("The only required key. The site's name, and the PDF's filename where none is given.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`version`, `author`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"1.0.0\"`, `\"\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Yours to use; nothing in the build reads them.")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("build", id: "build").h3

        Table(caption: "build") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("Default").header
                    Table.Cell("Meaning").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`output`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"./build\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Where the build is written, relative to the project.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`output_type`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"spa\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`spa`, `pdf`, `slides` or `elements` ([chapter 17](/docs/guide/outputs)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`ssg`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`false`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Pre-render every page to its own HTML ([chapter 17](/docs/guide/outputs)). Off, the build is one shell.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`split`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true`") }
                    Table.Cell("One JS and CSS chunk per page, loaded when its route shows.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`minify`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true`") }
                    Table.Cell("Strip comments and whitespace from the bundle and the sheet.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`sourcemap`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`false`") }
                    Table.Cell("Write a source map beside the bundle.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`compress`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Write a `.gz` beside every text output over a kilobyte, for a host that serves it.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`runtime`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"auto\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`auto` ships the runtime modules the program reaches; `full` ships all of them, for a program the scan cannot see (a `WF.*` call in a hand-written script).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`csp`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`false` for an existing project, `true` for one `wf init` writes") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Emit the Content-Security-Policy, and hold the output to it ([chapter 19](/docs/guide/security#content-security-policy)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`base_path`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Serve from a sub-path, such as `/docs` on GitHub Pages.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`budget`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\{\}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A gzipped size each named output should stay under: `\{ \"app.js\": \"40 kB\" \}`. Over it the build warns; it never fails.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`elements`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`[]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("With `output_type: \"elements\"`, the components to publish as custom elements ([chapter 8](/docs/guide/components#publishing-yours)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`media`") }
                    Table.Cell("see above")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The image pipeline: the `formats` and `widths` to write, the `quality` to encode at, and `pipeline: false` to copy files as `public/` always did ([chapter 16](/docs/guide/content)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`pdf`, `slides`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("see [chapter 17](/docs/guide/outputs)") }
                    Table.Cell("Page size, margins, fonts, and for a deck the slide size, chrome and colours.")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("dev, motion", id: "dev-motion").h3

        Table(caption: "dev, motion") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("Default").header
                    Table.Cell("Meaning").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`dev.port`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`3000`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The port `wf serve` listens on.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`dev.hot_reload`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true`") }
                    Table.Cell("Rebuild on a save, reload every open tab, and draw a failed build's error over the page.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`motion.duration`") }
                    Table.Cell("unset")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("How long an animation runs when the element does not say: `\"180ms\"`. It is the `animation-duration-normal` token ([chapter 13](/docs/guide/motion#the-defaults)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`motion.easing`") }
                    Table.Cell("unset")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("How it is paced: a CSS timing function, or `\"$ease-standard\"` to take a token's value.") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("meta", id: "meta").h3

        Table(caption: "meta") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("Default").header
                    Table.Cell("Meaning").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`title`, `description`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("What a page falls back to when it declares none.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`lang`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"en\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The document's language. An RTL locale sets `dir=\"rtl\"` on its own.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`site_url`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The site's absolute address. **Without it the tags that need one are left out rather than guessed at** — a wrong canonical URL costs more than a missing one ([chapter 16](/docs/guide/content)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`site_name`, `image`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("The name and the sharing image a link preview shows.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`favicon`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("The icon a tab shows.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`sitemap`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Write `sitemap.xml` and `robots.txt`.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`fonts`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`[]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Web-font stylesheets to link, each with a `preconnect` to its origin.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`stylesheets`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`[]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Extra stylesheets to link ahead of `styles.css` — a file in `public/`, or a URL. For what no element-level `style \{ \}` can say.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`integrity`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\{\}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The subresource-integrity hash of an external asset, by its URL ([chapter 19](/docs/guide/security#another-origins-files)).") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("theme, i18n, env", id: "theme-i18n-env").h3

        Table(caption: "theme, i18n, env") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("Default").header
                    Table.Cell("Meaning").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`theme.name`") }
                    Table.Cell("unset")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Which `theme` declaration to use. A project with exactly one need not name it.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`theme.dark`") }
                    Table.Cell("unset")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The theme that stands in under `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, and when the reader asks ([chapter 12](/docs/guide/styling)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`theme.builtin`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"full\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`full` ships the baseline design of the built-ins; `structural` only their layout and mechanics, for a project supplying its own.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`theme.tokens`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\{\}`") }
                    Table.Cell("Token overrides on top of the theme, for a value a machine supplies.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`i18n.default_locale`, `i18n.locales`, `i18n.dir`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"en\"`, `[\"en\"]`, `\"src/translations\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Translations ([chapter 15](/docs/guide/i18n)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`env`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\{\}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Values read in code as `env.NAME`, fixed at build time.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`public_env`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`[]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The `env` names a page may read, beyond every name beginning `PUBLIC_`. **Anything a page reads is in the bundle**, so this list is the difference between a setting and a leak ([chapter 19](/docs/guide/security#env-and-what-ends-up-in-the-bundle)).") }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
